# Expectation record for 04_bitfields/002_read_write.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_read_write
area               = 04_bitfields
description        = Reading and writing each bitfield of a struct, folded and volatile-runtime
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every value stored into a bitfield is inside the range its declared width and signedness
can represent, so no store is out of range and no implementation-defined truncation is
relied upon.  Each field carries an explicit signed or unsigned base type.  No padding bit
or padding byte is ever read.  Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
folded_read u3=6 s5=-13 u9=400 s12=-1500 u1=1
folded_write u3=1 s5=15 u9=511 s12=2047 u1=0
folded_min s5=-16 s12=-2048
runtime_read u3=6 s5=-13 u9=400 s12=-1500 u1=1
runtime_write u3=7 s5=-12 u9=401 s12=-1499 u1=0
runtime_max u3=7 u9=511 u1=1
runtime_zero u3=0 u9=0 u1=0
END
